{"records":[{"id":"vx9n7ckvz9","title":"Data for: Expression of the autophagy-related protein LC3B in granulosa cells, cumulus cells, and oocyte during atresia of pig antral follicles","description":"For a long time, in mammals apoptosis was accepted as the type of programmed cell death (PCD) occurring in ovarian follicles undergoing atresia. However, recent studies have suggested that autophagy may be an alternative mechanism involved in follicle depletion, working independently or in tandem with apoptosis. This research investigated by western blot and immunofluorescence the expression of LC3B protein in freshly collected granulosa cells (GC), cumulus cells (CC), and oocytes to evaluate the involvement of autophagy in the process of antral follicle atresia in sexually mature sows. Moreover, apoptosis was analysed by Annexin V and TUNEL assays in the same cellular cohorts to evaluate the correlation between the two processes. Our immunostaining results show that the autophagy is induced in the majority of GC, CC, and oocytes collected from early and advanced atretic follicles. The quantitative results of western blot analysis demonstrate that a significant (P < 0.05) and progressive increase of expression of the autophagy-related form of the protein (LC3B-II) takes places in these cells compared to healthy ones. Moreover, our data confirm that apoptosis occurs in GC of atretic follicles, thus showing that in pigs, like in rats and humans, apoptosis and autophagy work in GC as combined processes of PCD for antral follicle depletion. Finally, we did not detect apoptosis whereas we detected an overexpression of LC3B-II in CC and oocytes of atretic follicles, thus we suggest that in these cells the two processes work independently, with autophagy acting like a cytoprotective rather than a PCD mechanism.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-11-13T11:13:43.614Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vx9n7ckvz9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vx9n7ckvz9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Luisa","last_name":"Gioia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alessia","last_name":"Gloria","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luca","last_name":"Valbonetti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alberto","last_name":"Contri","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alessandro","last_name":"Colapietro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Claudio","last_name":"Festuccia","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oocyte","Apoptosis","Ovarian Follicle","Autophagy","Pig","Female Animal Reproduction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztt67dj7jr","title":"LC3B-II fluorescence intensity in PBMCs from healthy donors and ME/CFS patients (CTRL + wIRA)","description":"Our research hypothesis was that heat treatment by water-filtered infrared-A (wIRA) irradiation  at 39 °C for 60 minutes would lead to increased autophagy compared to an untreated control. The following files show the treatment of PBMCs from healthy donors and ME/CFS patients. The files show the median fluorescence intensity from healthy donors and ME/CFS patients as triplicates in untreated (CTRL) and treated (wIRA) cells. Or the mean values of the triplicates of each condition to compare healthy donors with ME/CFS patients. The data show a higher level of LC3B-II in ME/CFS patients compared to healthy donors and an increase in autophagy due to treatment with wIRA.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-24T18:53:27.466Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztt67dj7jr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztt67dj7jr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Hochecker","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autophagy","Heat Treatment"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zff84dy5ht","title":"Sequencing Data for \"Unbiased RNA Degrader Identification (URID) Uncovers an LC3B-Recruiting Chimera for COL15A1 mRNA Degradation\"","description":"An RNA-binding fragment (F1) with a mapped transcriptome-wide binding profile was conjugated to the LC3B ligand ispinesib to generate the degrader F1-ispinesib.  Integration of RNA-seq and Chemical Cross-Linking and Isolation by Pull-down combined with NGS Sequencing (Chem-CLIP-seq) data identified collagen type XV alpha 1 chain (COL15A1) mRNA as a target selectively degraded by F1-Ispinesib.  Other full plasmid sequencing files are also included here.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-19T10:33:29.729Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zff84dy5ht","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zff84dy5ht"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lexie","last_name":"Su","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chemical Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dxv5bfjz8s","title":"Data for: The PERK/Nrf2 pathway mediates endoplasmic reticulum stress-induced injury by upregulating endoplasmic reticulophagy in H9c2 cardiomyoblasts","description":"ERS caused ER-phagy with a decrease in cell viability and an increase in cell death in H9c2 cardiomyoblasts.(A) Live/Dead staining of H9c2 cardiomyoblasts was performed using a LIVE/DEAD® Viability/Cytotoxicity Kit (bar=40 μm, n=3). TG- or TM-treated H9c2 cardiomyoblasts were incubated with calcein-AM (green) and EthD-1 (red) for 10 min, and the fluorescence was visualized using a confocal microscope. Green fluorescence indicates live cells, while red fluorescence indicates dead cells. (E) Ultrastructural lesions in the ER caused by TG or TM and the occurrence of ER-phagy were observed via transmission electron microscopy (bar=500 nm). (F) Representative confocal microscopy images corresponding to analysis of ER-phagy for TG- or TM-treated H9c2 cardiomyoblasts and showing colocalization of autophagosomes with ER-fragments (bar=15 μm; n=3). LC3B was labeled with Texas Red, while calreticulin was labeled with Alexa Fluor 488. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-17T15:29:53.981Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dxv5bfjz8s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dxv5bfjz8s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiuhua","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autophagy","Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress"],"journal_issn":["00243205"]},{"id":"p733v4y76c","title":"The E3 ubiquitin ligase RNF10 modifies 40S ribosomal subunits of ribosomes compromised in translation","description":"Reversible monoubiquitination of small subunit ribosomal proteins RPS2 and RPS3 has been noted to occur on ribosomes involved in ZNF598-dependent mRNA surveillance. Subsequent deubiquitination of RPS2 and RPS3 by USP10 is critical for recycling of stalled ribosomes in a process known as ribosome-associated quality control (RQC). Here, we identified and characterized the RPS2- and RPS3-specific E3 ligase RING finger protein 10 (RNF10) and its role in translation. Overexpression of RNF10 increased 40S ribosomal subunit degradation similar in extent to the knockout of USP10. Although a substantial fraction of RNF10-mediated RPS2 and RPS3 monoubiquitination resulted from ZNF598-dependent sensing of collided ribosomes, ZNF598-independent impairment of translation initiation and elongation also contributed to RPS2 and RPS3 monoubiquitination. PAR-CLIP-based identification of crosslinked mRNA and tRNAs suggests recruitment of RNF10 to fully assembled, though functionally impaired or stalled ribosomes. These impeded ribosomes are tagged by ubiquitin at their 40S subunit for subsequent programmed degradation unless rescued by USP10. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-21T05:12:46.691Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p733v4y76c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p733v4y76c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aitor","last_name":"Garzia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cindy","last_name":"Meyer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Tuschl","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences","Biochemistry","Ribosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j99yg4nttw","title":"Streptococcus suis serotype 2 evades autophagic clearance via mitochondrial ROS-induced LMP and ATP6V1B1 downregulation","description":"Streptococcus suis serotype 2 (SS2) is an emerging zoonotic pathogen that causes severe infections with high mortality, yet its immune evasion mechanisms remain incompletely understood. Here, we found that SS2 infection induced autophagic flux blockade, as evidenced by accumulation of LC3-II and p62 and formation of non functional autolysosomes. Mechanistic studies revealed that SS2 infection caused mitochondrial damage, characterized by ROS burst, membrane potential loss, and ATP depletion. Excessive ROS triggered lysosomal membrane permeabilization (LMP) and downregulated ATP6V1B1, synergistically impairing lysosomal acidification, blocking autophagic flux, and promoting SS2 survival. Overexpression of ATP6V1B1 restored lysosomal pH, Cathepsin D activity, and autophagic flux, and reduced bacterial survival. SS2 also disrupted the host compensatory response by accelerating TFEB degradation via the ubiquitin‑proteasome pathway. In vivo, inhibition of lysosomal acidification exacerbates SS2‑induced mortality, organ bacterial loads and tissue damage, whereas ATP6V1B1 overexpression confers protection. This study reveals the molecular mechanism by which SS2 hijacks the host autophagic system, providing a theoretical foundation for developing host-directed anti-SS2 strategies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-06T13:56:00.648Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j99yg4nttw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j99yg4nttw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fei","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuzhi","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pei","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianda","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luogang","last_name":"Ding","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yulin","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhi","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenbo","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiang","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiaqiang","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bacteria","Pathogenesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tpjcr237pz","title":"Linares et al, Molecular Cell 2021","description":"The interferon (IFN) pathway is critical for cytotoxic T cell activation, which is central to tumor immunosurveillance and successful immunotherapy. We demonstrate here that PKCl/i inactivation results in the hyper-stimulation of the IFN cascade and the enhanced recruitment of CD8+ T cells that impaired the growth of intestinal tumors. PKCl/i directly phosphorylates and represses the activity of ULK2, promoting its degradation through an endosomal microautophagy-driven ubiquitin-dependent mechanism. Loss of PKCl/i results in increased levels of enzymatically active ULK2 that by direct phosphorylation activates TBK1 to foster the activation of the STING-mediated IFN response. PKCl/i inactivation also triggers autophagy that prevents STING degradation by chaperone-mediated autophagy. Thus, PKCl/i is a hub regulating the IFN pathway and three autophagic mechanisms that serve to maintain its homeostatic control. Importantly, single cell multiplex imaging and bioinformatics analysis demonstrated that low PKCl/i levels correlate with enhanced IFN signaling and good prognosis in colorectal cancer patients. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-20T19:31:36.433Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tpjcr237pz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tpjcr237pz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Moscat","last_name":"Diaz-Meco","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autophagy","Immunity","Colorectal Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n9txt6y5cj","title":"TRABID deubiquitinase overexpression enables synthetic lethality to PARP inhibitor via prolonged retention of 53BP1 at DSB sites","description":"Here, we demonstrate that TRABID deubiquitinase (DUB) binds to 53BP1 at endogenous level and regulates 53BP1 retention at DSB sites. TRABID deubiquitinates K29-linked polyubiquitination of 53BP1 mediated by E3 ubiquitin ligase SPOP and prevents 53BP1 dissociation from DSBs, consequently inducing HR defects and chromosomal instability. Prostate cancer cells with TRABID overexpression exhibit a high sensitivity to poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitors. This dataset include all the raw data of western blot from this project.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-27T11:33:09.418Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n9txt6y5cj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n9txt6y5cj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jian","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vnvrbrnz54","title":"PKR inhibitor alleviated demyelination through modulating autophagy via the AMPK/mTOR/STAT3 axis","description":"New folder: Immunohistochemistry images of AMPK in cerebellum\nNew folder.1: Immunohistochemistry images of COX4 in cerebrum and cerebellum\nNew folder.2: Immunohistochemistry images of P62 and LC3B in cerebellum\nNew folder.3: Immunohistochemistry images of MBP in cortex, corpus callosum and cerebellum\nNew folder.4: Immunohistochemistry images of mTOR in cerebrum and cerebellum\nNew folder.5: Immunofluorescence images of occludin in small intestine\nNew folder.6: Immunohistochemistry images of PKR in cerebrum and cerebellum\nNew folder.7: Immunohistochemistry images of STAT3 in cerebrum and cerebellum\nNew folder.8: Data of mouse body weight, behavioral tests, immunohistochemistry analysis and intestinal immunofluorescence analysis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-07T06:09:42.947Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vnvrbrnz54","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vnvrbrnz54"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lin","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Duo","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Meiliang ","last_name":"Pan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinyue","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei ","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Weiqiang","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia ","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lili ","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jijing ","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yixin ","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mingwei ","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yongbo ","last_name":"Kang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Weiping ","last_name":"Fan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lin ","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hongyan ","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroscience","Neuroimmunology","Cerebellar Cortex"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fk97ybvz8r","title":"Orignal data: USP10 strikes down β-catenin by dual-wielding deubiquitinase activity and phase separation potential","description":"Western blot and gel source data of the article entitled \"USP10 strikes down β-catenin by dual-wielding deubiquitinase activity and phase separation potential\". The data was sorted by figure panels. Each tab in the excel file contains the data from an indicated figure panel. All images are the original scans and the sizes are draggable. Both chemiluminescence and bright field images are provided.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-23T11:06:16.56Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fk97ybvz8r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fk97ybvz8r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ren","last_name":"Sheng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Gel"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":136561,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":107,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":136561,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":135887,"restricted":70,"embargoed":604},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2298},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":700},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":477},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":434},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":424},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":398},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":340},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":279},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":228}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":133912},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1904},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-f